%S A402540 1,2,1,2,2,2,1,2,1,1,3,1,2,1,2,2,2,1,2,1,1,3,1,3,1,2,2,2,1,3,1,3,1,1,
%T A402540 2,1,2,2,2,1,1,2,1,3,1,2,1,3,2,2,1,1,2,1,3,2,2,1,2,2,3,1,2,1,3,1,1,2,
%U A402540 1,1,2,2,1,3,1,2,1,3,1,2,1,2,1,2,2,1,2,1,3,1,2,1,3,2,2,1,3,1,3,2
%N A402540 Number of primes p such that p*(p+1) divides A126798(n).
%C A402540 Let f(m) be the number of primes p such that p*(p+1) divides m.
%C A402540 This sequence is f(A126798(n)).
%C A402540 A126798 lists exactly the positive integers m for which f(m)>0.
%C A402540 Every positive integer occurs infinitely often as a value of f(m).
%C A402540 For every k>=1, f((prime(k)+1)!)=k.
%e A402540 A126798(1)=6, so a(1)=1 because only p=2 satisfies p*(p+1) divides 6.
%e A402540 A126798(2)=12, so a(2)=2 because p=2 and p=3 satisfy the condition.
%e A402540 A126798(11)=60, so a(11)=3 because p=2, 3 and 5 satisfy the condition.
%o A402540 (PARI)
%o A402540 a(n)={my(m=0,j=0,t);while(j<n,m++;t=0;forprime(p=2,(sqrtint(4*m+1)-1)\2,if(m%(p*(p+1))==0,t++));if(t,j++));t}
%Y A402540 Cf. A126798 (indices of nonzero terms of the original function).
%K A402540 nonn,new
%O A402540 1,2
%A A402540 _Nguyen Trong Nhan_, Nov 20 2026
